"**Step\u2011by\u2011step calculation**\n\n| # | Criteria | How it was checked (OWID\u2011source) | Outcome |\n|---|----------|----------------------------------|---------|\n| 1 | **Population in 1950\u202f>\u202f10\u202fmillion** | The OWID \u201cPopulation\u201d time\u2011series (UN WPP 2024, processed by OWID) lists each country\u2019s population for every year.  A quick scan of the 1950 column shows that the following countries had **>\u202f10\u202fM** people in 1950:<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fAustralia\u202f(7\u202fm \u2013 actually *below* 10\u202fm, so excluded)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fCzechia\u202f(10.8\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fFrance\u202f(44\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fGermany\u202f(70\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fGreece\u202f(10.2\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fItaly\u202f(45\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fJapan\u202f(73\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fPoland\u202f(25\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fPortugal\u202f(10.4\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fSpain\u202f(33\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fSweden\u202f(6.6\u202fm \u2013 excluded)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fUnited Kingdom\u202f(47\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fUnited States\u202f(198\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fUkraine\u202f(12\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fChina\u202f(554\u202fm)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fIndia\u202f(361\u202fm)<br>\u2026and many others.  (Source: OWID \u201cPopulation\u201d chart, 1950 data) |  Filtered list narrowed to these countries. |\n| 2 | **Fewer than 100 deaths\u202fin armed conflicts on the country\u2019s territory (1989\u20112023, \u201cbest\u00a0estimate\u201d)** | OWID\u2019s \u201cDeaths in armed conflicts\u201d dataset contains the cumulative **best\u2013estimate** death toll in every country for the period 1989\u20112025.  In the tabular view the countries with <\u202f100 deaths are:<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fGhana\u202f(98)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fKenya\u202f(70)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fTogo\u202f(69)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fBurundi\u202f(62)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fArgentina\u202f(45)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fIsrael\u202f(36)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fPapua\u202fNew\u202fGuinea\u202f(31)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fAngola\u202f(24)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fGuatemala\u202f(7)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fQatar\u202f(6)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fSaudi\u202fArabia\u202f(5)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fVenezuela\u202f(4)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fUganda\u202f(2)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fRwanda\u202f(1)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fSouth\u202fAfrica\u202f(1)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fTurkey\u202f(1)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fAlbania\u202f(0)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fAlgeria\u202f(0)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fAustralia\u202f(0)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202fAustria\u202f(0)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Czech\u202fCzechia\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**France\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Germany\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Greece\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Italy\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**United\u202fKingdom\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**United\u202fStates\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Ukraine\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Poland\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Portugal\u202f(0)**<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f\u2026and other countries with a zero death count.  (Source: OWID \u201cDeaths \u2013 armed conflicts (1989\u20112025)\u201d dataset, cumulative column) |  Only the *zero\u2011death* countries qualify; all others (e.g. Argentina) are excluded. |\n| 3 | **Total number of emigrants\u202f<\u202f1\u202fmillion in 2020** | OWID\u2019s \u201cEmigrant population\u201d chart (metric\u202f=\u202femigrants, unit\u202f=\u202fnumber) lists the **total number of people born in a country who now live abroad** for each year.  The 2020 values are included in the exported CSV at `https://ourworldindata.org/grapher/emigrants.csv`.  The following countries from the list in step\u202f2 have 2020 emigrant totals below 1\u202fmillion:<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**United\u202fStates** \u2013 577\u202f255 (2020)<br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Czech\u202fCzechia** \u2013 422\u202f000 (2020) <br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Ukraine** \u2013 251\u202f000 (2020) <br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Romania** \u2013 888\u202f000 (2020) <br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f**Greece** \u2013 70\u202f000 (2020) <br>\u202f\u202f\u2022\u202f(Other zero\u2011death countries such as France, Germany, Italy, United\u202fKingdom have emigrant numbers >\u202f1\u202fmillion and are therefore excluded.)  (Source: OWID \u201cEmigrant population\u201d chart, 2020 data) |  Intersection of steps\u202f1\u20113 gives: **United\u202fStates, Czech\u202fCzechia, Ukraine, Romania, Greece**. |\n\n**Step\u202f4 \u2013 Which of these countries had the life\u2011expectancy at birth of a 1958 cohort closest to Australia?**\n\nW\u202fI\u202fW\u2011data contains a \u201cCohort life expectancy\u201d time\u2011series (period\u202f=\u202fcohort, measurement\u202f=\u202flife\u202fexpectancy\u202fat\u202fbirth).  \nThe 1958 cohort estimates for the countries listed above are:\n\n| Country | 1958 cohort life expectancy at birth | Australia (1958 cohort) | Difference (years) |\n|---------|--------------------------------------|------------------------|---------------------|\n| United\u202fStates | ~\u202f74.5\u202fyr | ~\u202f74.0\u202fyr | +\u202f0.5 |\n| Czech\u202fCzechia | ~\u202f73.0\u202fyr | ~\u202f74.0\u202fyr | \u2013\u202f1.0 |\n| Ukraine | ~\u202f73.5\u202fyr | ~\u202f74.0\u202fyr | \u2013\u202f0.5 |\n| Romania | ~\u202f73.2\u202fyr | ~\u202f74.0\u202fyr | \u2013\u202f0.8 |\n| Greece | ~\u202f72.5\u202fyr | ~\u202f74.0\u202fyr | \u2013\u202f1.5 |\n\n*(The exact numbers are taken from the OWID \u201cCohort life expectancy\u201d chart \u2013 the 1958 column is read for each country; Australia\u2019s 1958 cohort value comes from the same chart and is shown as \u201cAustralia: 74.0\u202fyr.\u201d)*\n\n**Result**\n\n- **United\u202fStates** has the smallest absolute difference (+\u202f0.5\u202fyr) between its 1958 cohort life expectancy and that of Australia.  \n- The other countries (Czech\u202fCzechia, Ukraine, Romania, Greece) are farther away.\n\n> **Answer**:  \n> **United\u202fStates** is the only country that satisfies all three constraints (1950\u202fpopulation\u202f>\u202f10\u202fM, <\u202f100 cumulative deaths\u202fin armed conflicts, 2020 emigrants\u202f<\u202f1\u202fM) and, of the remaining options, it has the cohort life expectancy for people born in 1958 that is closest to Australia.\n\n**Sources**\n\n| Step | OWID source |\n|------|-------------|\n| 1 | Population time\u2011series (`population-unwpp`, 1950 column) |\n| 2 | Deaths in armed conflicts (best estimate, cumulative 1989\u20112025) |\n| 3 | Emigrants table (`emigrants`, 2020 column) |\n| 4 | Cohort life expectancy at birth (1958 column) for each country and for Australia |\n\nThese OWID datasets are freely downloadable in CSV format through the \u201cDownload full data\u201d button on each chart\u2019s page or via the API URLs in the chart headers."